\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The compact Spin cover is universal from dimension three}

\p{For #{n\geq3}, the covering in
\ref{fcap-0039} is the universal covering of #{SO(n)}. The
proof uses the action on the unit sphere from \ref{fcap-0024}, together
with the topology just supplied.}

\p{Fix a unit vector #{e}. Its stabilizer in #{\Spin(Q_n^+)} is the
preimage of #{SO(e^\perp)}. The Clifford map for the orthogonal inclusion
#{e^\perp\hookrightarrow\mathbb R^n} embeds
#{\Spin(Q_{n-1}^+)} into that preimage, maps onto
#{SO(e^\perp)}, and has the same kernel #{\{1,-1\}}. Hence the embedded
subgroup is exactly the stabilizer. It is closed. Using the concrete Lie
group structure of \ref{fcap-0037}, the closed-subgroup
bundle theorem gives

##{\Spin(n-1)\longrightarrow\Spin(n)\longrightarrow S^{n-1}.}

This supplies the stabilizer step rather than inferring a fibration from
transitivity alone.}

\p{One can see the local section directly in the positive-square
convention. For a unit vector #{x\ne-e}, put

##{u(x)=\frac{e+x}{\sqrt{Q_n^+(e+x)}},\qquad
s(x)=\iota(u(x))\iota(e).}

Positive definiteness makes the denominator nonzero, and both vectors
have square one. Thus #{s(x)} is even with reverse norm one. If
#{h=\iota(e+x)}, the Clifford relation gives

##{h\iota(e)h=\bigl(2+B_Q(e,x)\bigr)\iota(x)
=Q_n^+(e+x)\iota(x),}

so #{s(x)\iota(e)s(x)^{-1}=\iota(x)}. The formula is continuous on
#{S^{n-1}\setminus\{-e\}}. In TauCeti #{e} is the last coordinate
vector: \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.realCliffordSpinLastLocalSection_apply}
records the formula,
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.continuousOn_realCliffordSpinLastLocalSection}
its continuity on that punctured sphere, and
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.realCliffordSpinLastLocalSection_action}
its orbit identity. The definition assigns the identity at #{-e}; this
totalization is not a continuous section on the whole sphere.}

\p{With #{k=n-1\geq1},
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.realCliffordSpinContinuousMulEquivLastStabilizer}
identifies #{\Spin(k)} with the entire last-vector stabilizer in
#{\Spin(k+1)}, continuously and multiplicatively.
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.exists_realCliffordSpinOrbitTrivialization}
then provides a bundle chart around every sphere point #{y}, with base
#{S^k\setminus\{-y\}} and fiber #{\Spin(k)}; the resulting fiber-bundle
statement is
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.isFiberBundle_realCliffordSpinOrbitMap}.
These are charts for the actual orbit map, with the same rank shift as
the displayed bundle.}

\p{For #{n=3}, \ref{fcap-002H} gives the coordinate isomorphism with
unit quaternions. Its displayed linear map and inverse restrict
continuously to the unit three-sphere, so #{\Spin(3)} is simply
connected. For #{n\geq4}, the homotopy exact sequence of the bundle and
simple connectedness of #{S^{n-1}} give a surjection

##{\pi_1\Spin(n-1)\longrightarrow\pi_1\Spin(n).}

Induction therefore gives #{\pi_1\Spin(n)=0} for every #{n\geq3}.
Together with path-connectedness and the two-sheeted covering, this makes
#{\Spin(n)\to SO(n)} the universal cover.}

\p{The formal base case uses
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.realSpinThreeContinuousMulEquivQuaternionUnitary},
the continuous multiplicative quaternion equivalence corresponding to
\ref{fcap-002H}. The full conclusion
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.simplyConnectedSpace_realCliffordSpinGroupZero}
establishes simple connectedness for every #{n\geq3}. Its induction
uses the two antipodal bundle charts: their bases are contractible
punctured spheres, their fibers are simply connected by induction, and
their overlap is path-connected. Van Kampen then gives simple
connectedness of the total space. This implements the same geometric
induction through charts rather than the homotopy exact sequence used
above. The quaternion case starts the induction; #{\Spin(2)} is not a
simply connected base case. Combining this conclusion with
\ref{fcap-0039} yields the universal-cover assertion.}

\p{\citet{Section 1.8, pp. 40--42}{gallier2014clifford} gives this
sphere--stabilizer induction.
\citet{Section 1.6, Lemma 1.4, p. 13}{meinrenken2013clifford}
independently checks the base topology using the parallel sphere fibration
for #{SO(n)}. The explicit
carrier/stabilizer comparison above is the convention bridge to the
selected positive-square model.}

\p{The lower ranks remain separate:

##{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
n&\Spin(n)&\text{cover behavior}\\ \hline
0&\{1\}&\text{separate trivial carrier}\\
1&\{1,-1\}&\text{two sheets over }SO(1),\ \text{not universal}\\
2&S^1&\text{twofold circle cover of }SO(2),\ \pi_1\ne0.
\end{array}}

The rank-two identification follows from
#{a+b e_1e_2} with #{a=\cos\phi} and #{b=\sin\phi}. In the orientation of
\ref{fcap-0038}, its vector action has angle #{-2\phi}; hence the
map is a twofold circle cover. The formal simple-connectedness theorem
therefore has exactly the lower bound needed here; the universal-cover
claim combines it with the separately established covering.}
